# ============================================================================
# ASTERIA LOOM — ADVANCED overlay: shaders.properties FRAGMENT
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file is NOT a properties file Iris ever loads on its own. It is appended
# to shaders/shaders.properties (verbatim, always current) by
# tools/overlay_props.py, which both tools/package.py and tools/validate.py call,
# so the Advanced zip and the compile gate see byte-identical text.
#
# WHY AN APPEND AND NOT A REPLACEMENT: shaders.properties describes the WHOLE
# pack — five profiles, nine screens, the slider list, every blend/size/program
# directive. An overlay COPY of it would have to restate all of that to add the
# two lines below, and would then go stale the moment anyone edits the canonical
# file: the default zip would ship the new profile/screen, the Advanced zip
# would keep serving a months-old one, and NOTHING would error. Only the delta
# lives here; the canonical file stays the single source of truth.
#
# Duplicate-key rule: .properties keeps the LAST assignment, and this fragment
# is last, so a key repeated here overrides the base. Prefer adding keys.
# ============================================================================

# ---- Capability gate: this build REQUIRES compute -------------------------
# The Advanced zip ships a compute stage for the `final` pass in every world
# folder (final.csh — the histogram auto-exposure metering). Unlike every other
# feature in this pack, that is not a graceful-fallback situation: Iris compiles
# every .csh a pack ships, unconditionally, so a machine that cannot run compute
# cannot load this zip at all. Declaring the flag REQUIRED (rather than optional, as the canonical file
# does for the whole four-flag set) makes Iris refuse the pack up front with a
# clear capability message instead of failing deep inside shader compilation —
# and it is the machine-readable statement of "this archive is Windows/Linux
# only; macOS users want AsteriaLoom-<version>.zip".
#
# Token name: the Iris feature-flag identifier is COMPUTE_SHADERS (Iris then
# defines IRIS_FEATURE_COMPUTE_SHADERS in every program — the same spelling
# tools/validate.py injects for its `advanced` compile target). COMPUTE_SHADERS
# is also listed in the base file's `iris.features.optional`; naming it in both
# lists is harmless (they are separate sets — required is enforced, optional is
# only reported), and re-stating the optional list here just to remove one entry
# is exactly the stale-copy trap this fragment exists to avoid.
iris.features.required = COMPUTE_SHADERS

# ---- Auto-exposure metering ownership (no directive needed) ---------------
# NOTE, deliberately NOT a directive: composite14's fragment metering is NOT
# disabled here, and it must not be. There is no properties key that removes one
# MRT write from a pass — `program.composite14.enabled` would also kill the
# bloom-combine that pass exists for, and no GUI option gates the metering
# block (AL_EXPOSURE_* are internal defines a profile cannot touch). Instead the
# compute pass is made the sole *authority* structurally:
#   * final.csh is the `final` pass's COMPUTE stage, which Iris runs before
#     that pass's fragment and therefore after every composite; it overwrites
#     colortex5.a(0,0), the texel final.fsh reads a moment later, so the
#     fragment path's metered value is never displayed;
#   * the compute integrator keeps its OWN previous-exposure state in
#     colortex5.a(1,0), which composite14/composite1 pass through byte-exact —
#     so the fragment path's output is never fed back in either.
# The exposure that reaches the screen is therefore integrated exactly once, by
# the histogram pass. See each world's final.csh for the full argument (and for
# why leaving the fragment path alive is a feature: if a driver ever declines
# the compute dispatch, the pack degrades to the mip-average metering instead of
# to a frozen exposure).
